# [[strict|Strict]] [[adherence]] to a [[text]], especially to the [[Bible]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2021|author=Christopher Hancock|title=Christianity and Confucianism: Culture, Faith and Politics|publisher=Bloomsbury Publishing|isbn=9780567657688|page=328|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=xKYFEAAAQBAJ&pg=PA328&dq=%22textualism%22+bible&hl=&cd=11&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22textualism%22%20bible&f=false|text=First, building on the earlier work of philologists, historians, orientalists, and biblical scholars we have noted already, the late 19th and early 20th centuries witness growth and development of '''''textualism''''', in which words are seen—no, revered—as the referential embodiment of meaning and truth.}}
# {{lb|en|law}} A [[formalist]] legal theory that interprets based on the [[ordinary]] meaning of the legal text.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Andrei Marmor|title=Law in the Age of Pluralism|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=9780199745111|page=198|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=QDDTdVk-XWAC&pg=PA198&dq=%22textualism%22&hl=&cd=20&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22textualism%22&f=false|text=And this is the upshot of '''textualism''': textualists do not want judges to make the law. This, at least, is the official doctrine, and it sounds very democratic.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Adam Liptak|title=Supreme Court Limits E.P.A.’s Ability to Restrict Power Plant Emissions|work=The New York Times|date=2022-06-30|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/06/30/us/epa-carbon-emissions-scotus.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=The Supreme Court’s conservative majority is generally committed to '''textualism''', a judicial approach that focuses on the words of the law as written rather than its larger purpose or the intentions of its drafters. In a 2015 appearance at Harvard Law School, Justice Kagan said that '''textualism''' had triumphed across the ideological spectrum. “We’re all textualists now,” she said then.}}
# [[textual criticism|Textual criticism]], especially that of the Bible.

